\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  of Malta\uc2  - Open day on the occasion of the anniversaries of the CBM, ESCB, ECB and Eurosystem\b0\par\par\uc2 1,270 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2008\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 Release date - 28052008\par \par \uc2 In 2008 the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malta\uc2  is celebrating its 40th anniversary while the European System of Central Banks (ESCB) and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) are commemorating their 10th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore the euro and the Eurosystem will be ten years old on 1 January 2009.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malta\uc2  1968-2008\par \par \uc2 The establishment of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Malta was first recommended in January 1964 by a UN mission headed by Professor Wolfgang F. Stolper on the grounds that there were serious gaps in Malta\u8217\'20\'19s financial infrastructure, particularly concerning advice to the Government on financial and monetary matters.\par \par \uc2 In response to this recommendation, the Maltese Government obtained technical advice from the Bank of England and the International Monetary Fund. In February 1967 a senior official of the Bank of England, Dr Philip L. Hogg, arrived in Malta to carry out the necessary organisational and legislative work. Legislation was enacted on 11 November 1967 and five months later, on 17 April 1968, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malta\uc2  was formally established. Dr Hogg became the first Governor of the Bank.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  history of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malta\uc2  until 2008 can be subdivided into three main periods (listed hereunder) which reflect the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s evolution over the years from an organization carrying out central banking functions in the context of Malta\u8217\'20\'19s fixed exchange rate regime to an institution which is now part of the Eurosystem, which sets and implements monetary policy in the fifteen countries of the euro area.\par \par \uc2 1968 - 1994\par \par \uc2 Following its establishment in 1968 the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malta\uc2  focused mainly on the implementation of exchange rate policy, the management of the country\u8217\'20\'19s reserves, the supervision of the banking system, the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the provision of banking services to the government, public sector bodies and banks.\par \par \uc2 1994 - 2002\par \par \uc2 This period was marked by a notable change in the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s policy orientation as part of an overall modernization of the financial services sector as reflected in the introduction of new legislation and an extensive revision to the existent laws. The Bank was granted greater autonomy for the formulation and implementation of monetary policy. A Monetary Policy Council was set up and the Bank launched its first open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a view to shifting from a system of direct monetary controls and administered interest rates to a more market-oriented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and market-driven interest rates.\par \par \uc2 2002 - 2007\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malta\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s preparations for European Union (EU) membership meant that the Bank had to adapt itself to a new environment in line with the country\u8217\'20\'19s new commitments. The Bank was granted full independence to formulate and implement monetary policy. When Malta joined the EU the Bank became part of the ESCB. The Governor of the Bank began to sit on the General Council of the ECB and experts from the Bank became members of ESCB committees.\par \par \uc2 On 1 January 2008 Malta joined the euro area and the euro substituted the lira as Malta\u8217\'20\'19s national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malta\uc2  joined the Eurosystem and was integrated within the decision making bodies of the ECB. The Governor became a member of the Governing Council of the ECB and experts from the Bank started to participate in Eurosystem committees. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malta\uc2  Act was brought in line with Eurosystem requirements.\par \par \uc2 The ESCB and the ECB 1998-2008\par \par \uc2 The history of the ESCB and of the ECB is closely linked with the evolution of Economic and Monetary Union (EMU) in Europe.\par \par \uc2 On the basis of the Delors report the European Council, in 1989, decided that the first phase of EMU, which consisted of the dismantling of all internal barriers to the free movement of persons, goods, services and capital within Europe should start on 1 July 1990. At the same time negotiations began on the Treaty on the European Union, or the Maastricht Treaty, which was signed in 1992 and entered into force on 1 November 1993.\par \par \uc2 In January 1994 the second phase of EMU began with the creation of the European Monetary Institute (EMI). This phase covered the technical preparations for the introduction of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the control of excessive deficits and the achievement of convergence in the economic and monetary policies of participating Member States.\par \par \uc2 Following agreements on the Stability and Growth Pact and on a new exchange rate mechanism (ERM II), the Council of the European Union, in May 1998, decided that 11 EU Member States (Belgium, Germany, Spain, France, Ireland, Italy, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Austria, Portugal and Finland) had met the convergence criteria to enable them to participate in the third and final stage of EMU and adopt the euro as their national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In June 1998 the EMI was liquidated and the ECB was set up in Frankfurt am Main, Germany, under the presidency of Willem F Duisenberg. At the same time the ESCB, which comprises the ECB and the national central banks (NCBs) of all EU Member States, also began to function. The third stage of EMU commenced on 1 January 1999 with the introduction of the euro as the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its virtual form. The concept of the Eurosystem was also introduced concurrently with the introduction of the euro. The Eurosystem is made up of the ECB and the NCBs of euro area Member States.\par \par \uc2 Greece joined the euro area in 2001 and, a year later, the euro banknotes and coins were put in circulation, making the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a reality to the European public. In 2003 Jean-Claude Trichet was appointed as President of the ECB. The euro area continued to expand with the inclusion of Slovenia in 2007 and Cyprus and Malta in 2008, bringing the total number of participating countries to 15.\par \par \uc2 Throughout their ten years of existence the ESCB and the Eurosystem had a major role in ensuring that Europe and, especially the euro area, benefited from stability in prices and interest rates, sustained GDP growth and low unemployment. They were also instrumental in safeguarding the integrity of the financial system and facilitating payment services in a world which has become more integrated. The euro has firmly established itself as a major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Anniversary events\par \par \uc2 The ESCB is commemorating its 10th anniversary through various activities organized by the ECB in collaboration with the various NCBs. Among other activities the ECB has organized a series of Cultural Days in Frankfurt, covering a range of events involving artists from the various EU countries. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malta\uc2  has contributed to these Cultural Days in various ways, including the lending of paintings from its collection for an exhibition at the ECB\u8217\'20\'19s premises and the co-financing with other NCBs of a concert. Staff members from the Bank have also participated in sports events which have been organized for ESCB employees.\par \par \uc2 Open Day\par \par \uc2 To mark the anniversaries, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malta\uc2 , in line with other EU NCBs, is holding an Open Day on Sunday 1 June 2008 from 09:00 to 12:30 at its main building in Pjazza Kastilja, Valletta. The public will have the opportunity to visit the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exhibition and the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s Boardroom where various memorabilia are being displayed. Various information materials will also be distributed. A panel exhibition covering the history of the Bank as well as the history of EMU is also on display in the foyer.\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020080602e4620008l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
