\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Malta won't have to follow ECB if it hikes rates  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nelson Graves  \par \uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2005\par 11:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 VALLETTA, Nov 23 (Reuters) - Malta's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor expects the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates next week but said the island nation will not necessarily have to follow suit even though its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Michael Bonello, governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malta, told Reuters in an interview that sustained economic growth could eventually enable the European Union's smallest member state to cut its own benchmark rate.  \par \par \uc2 He said Malta was committed to trimming its budget deficit and debt before joining the euro in 2008, but it was not clear whether the EU would block new members from adopting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they failed narrowly to meet admission criteria.  \par \par \uc2 Bonello, without indicating any first-hand knowledge of the ECB's intentions, said he sensed the Frankfurt-based bank's Governing Council would tighten credit next week.  \par \par \uc2 "We now sense that the ECB is going to raise rates on the first of December," Bonello, 60, said on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 A 25-point rise in the ECB rate would leave a 100-basis-point gap with \b \highlight22\uc2 Malta\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised in April by 25 basis points to 3.25 percent.  \par \par \uc2 But Bonello, who has led the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1999, said he did not think a narrowing of the gap would spark an outflow from Malta's lira, which has been pegged to the euro since May.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think it will lower it by enough to make people think it is worth their while to go into a foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Malta's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent an estimated $300 million between September 2004 and March this year to support the lira before Malta fixed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 0.4293 per euro.  \par \par \uc2 But Bonello said that since May, Malta's reserves had increased by more than 100 million Maltese liri (230 million euros), roughly offsetting the preceding outflow.  \par \par \uc2 "Corporate customers now feel confident enough to take out loans in euros, which means they believe the (exchange) rate will stick," said the Oxford-educated Bonello, who previously worked for a local brewer and as a U.N. diplomat.  \par \par \uc2 'REALM OF RIDICULE'  \par \par \uc2 Yields on Malta's 10-year bonds, at about 4.4 percent, have started converging on the euro rate of 3.0 to 3.15 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Bonello said any move to bring Malta's short-term rate in line with the ECB would depend on whether it could generate a "sustainable recovery" in growth, which could ease the current account and budget deficits and reduce borrowing requirements.  \par \par \uc2 "But we have to remember that we succeeded in bringing money back partly because it was more advantageous for people to invest in Maltese liri assets than in euro assets," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Bonello said Malta would not have to reduce rates gradually.  \par \par \uc2 "You can wait if necessary and then just do it all in one go. One hundred basis points by recent standards is a lot maybe, but it's not in absolute terms. It's not impossible," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Bonello said Malta had adopted the fiscal discipline needed for it to meet its goal of adopting the euro by 2008.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation, forecast by the government to rise to 3.1 percent in 2006 from 2.8 percent this year, has to be watched but will not necessarily be so high as to disqualify Malta from joining the euro as oil price rises also hit other countries, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Prime Minister Lawrence Gonzi said oil-driven inflation could delay Malta's euro entry.  \par \par \uc2 Growth, however, will be crucial to Malta's aspirations to join the euro. The government forecasts modest GDP growth of about 1.5 percent this year, falling to 1.1 percent next year.  \par \par \uc2 "So if we make 1.1 percent growth, we are home and dry. If we make a little more, then it is a little easier. If not, we are in trouble," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Bonello said Europe's leaders would have to decide how strictly to apply the Maastricht criteria to the 10 new EU members seeking to join the euro. He noted that Slovenia, Lithuania and Estonia -- the three scheduled to join the euro first -- "are tending to have high inflation".  \par \par \uc2 "When they're having all these shenanigans with the Greeks, the Italians and everybody else, and the Portuguese, are they going to be sort of holier than thou and say, 'No, but you know it's got to be 3.0, it can't be 3.05 percent of GDP'?" he said.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we would get into a realm of ridicule there. What's so magical about this three percent anyway?"  \par \par \uc2 But he added Malta must stick to its fiscal targets.  \par \par \uc2 "Credit rating agencies think that there are one or two notches that will come down if we meet the targets, and we are close enough," he said.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-MALTA (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|BNX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051123e1bn000nl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
